//? Title: Tensors as Strided Views: Shapes, Strides, Broadcasting and When a Reshape Copies
//? Subtitle: The address formula behind every tensor, which operations share memory and which copy, the exact rule for a no-copy reshape, and zero-copy moving averages of the air-passenger series, using datamunge's nn::view_strides and nn::sliding_window
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A tensor is a buffer of numbers plus three pieces of metadata: a shape, strides and an offset. Element \((i_0,\dots,i_{r-1})\) lives at storage position
\(\text{offset}+\sum_k i_ks_k\). Transposing, slicing, broadcasting and windowing change only the metadata, so they cost a fraction of a microsecond at any size.
A copy costs time proportional to the data, about 10 ms for a \(4096\times4096\) float32 transpose in the run below. This report derives the exact condition under which a reshape
can share storage. Within each minimal group of axes that merge, the old strides must be contiguous among themselves. The library now applies that rule in `nn::reshape`, which previously copied
every non-contiguous input. On 20 000 random strided tensors and target shapes, the rule agrees with a brute-force search for any stride vector in every case.
The library also gained `nn::sliding_window` (a differentiable zero-copy unfold), `nn::may_share_memory`, `nn::can_view` and `nn::storage_bytes`.
On the monthly air-passenger series, a 13-month sliding window over 1152 bytes of storage gives the centred \(2\times12\) moving average without materialising its 13 728 bytes.
A reshape view gives the seasonal factors and pairs of slices give the autocorrelations, all matching R's `decompose` and `acf` to three decimals.
)

\toc

> The problem

A first look at any array library covers construction, shapes and dtypes, slicing, arithmetic with broadcasting, and the shape operations: reshape, transpose, concatenate
and stack. The useful question behind each one is whether it moves data. In `datamunge::nn`, as in NumPy and PyTorch, a tensor is a cheap handle to a shared
buffer (Harris et al., 2020; Paszke et al., 2019). Many operations return a new handle on the *same* buffer. That is fast and memory-free, but a write through one handle is then seen by the others.
Knowing which operations share memory, and why, is what makes the rest of the library predictable.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/tensor_basics.cpp`. That example printed each operation's result and
checked a few values. All of its computations reappear here, together with the metadata that explains them.

> The method

>> The address formula

A tensor of shape \((n_0,\dots,n_{r-1})\) with strides \((s_0,\dots,s_{r-1})\) and offset \(o\) reads element \(i=(i_0,\dots,i_{r-1})\) from storage position

$$
p(i)=o+\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}i_ks_k ,\qquad 0\le i_k<n_k .
$$
\alttext(p of i equals o plus the sum over k from 0 to r minus 1 of i k times s k, for i k from 0 to n k minus 1.)

A freshly created tensor is *contiguous* (row-major): \(s_{r-1}=1\) and \(s_k=n_{k+1}s_{k+1}\), so \(s_k=\prod_{j>k}n_j\). Each view operation is a rule for new metadata:

- *transpose* permutes the pairs \((n_k,s_k)\);
- *slicing* axis \(k\) from \(a\) with step \(h\) sets \(o\leftarrow o+as_k\), \(s_k\leftarrow hs_k\) and \(n_k\leftarrow\) the count;
- *broadcasting* a length-1 axis to length \(m\) sets its stride to 0, so all \(m\) positions read the same number;
- *a sliding window* of length \(w\) and step \(h\) along axis \(k\) replaces \(n_k\) by the number of windows \(\lfloor(n_k-w)/h\rfloor+1\) and \(s_k\) by \(hs_k\), and appends a new axis \((w,s_k)\).
  Consecutive windows then overlap in storage.

None of these touches the buffer, so each costs the same at every size.

>> When can a reshape be a view?

A reshape keeps the row-major order of the elements and changes the shape. Write \(p_0,p_1,\dots,p_{N-1}\) for the storage positions of the elements in that order. A view
with new shape \(m\) exists if some strides \(\sigma\) give \(o+\sum_lj_l\sigma_l=p_{L(j)}\), where \(L(j)\) is the row-major index of \(j\) in \(m\).
Ignore axes of length 1, whose strides never matter. Then split the old and new shapes into the shortest consecutive groups with equal products. For example,
\((4,6)\to(2,2,6)\) splits into \(\{4\}\to\{2,2\}\) and \(\{6\}\to\{6\}\).

*Sufficiency.* Suppose that within each old group every stride equals the next axis's length times the next stride. Then the group behaves as a single axis of length \(P\)
(the group product) and stride \(s_q\) (its last stride): its elements sit at \(s_q\cdot\ell\) for \(\ell=0,\dots,P-1\), relative to the group's origin. Any row-major split of that
axis is a view, with strides \(\sigma_l=s_q\prod_{l'>l,\,l'\in\text{group}}m_{l'}\).

*Necessity.* Suppose instead that two adjacent old axes in a group are not contiguous, \(s_k\ne n_{k+1}s_{k+1}\). The group is minimal, so some new axis in it spans the boundary between
old axes \(k\) and \(k+1\). Stepping along that axis must then move by a constant stride both within a run of axis \(k+1\) and across the end of that run, which the
mismatch forbids.

This is the rule NumPy applies before falling back to a copy. `nn::view_strides` implements it, `nn::can_view` reports it, and `nn::reshape` now uses it.
Before this change `reshape` copied whenever its input was not contiguous. `squeeze` and `expand_dims` are reshapes, so even adding a length-1 axis to a transpose
copied the whole tensor.

>> Broadcasting and dtypes

Two shapes broadcast by aligning them on the right. Each pair of lengths must be equal or contain a 1, and the result takes the larger. A length-1 or missing axis is read with
stride 0, so broadcasting never copies. Mixed dtypes promote along \(\text{bool}<\text{int32}<\text{int64}<\text{float32}<\text{float64}\). Casting a float to an integer
truncates toward zero. Float32 has a 24-bit significand, so it represents every integer up to \(2^{24}=16\,777\,216\), half of them between \(2^{24}\) and \(2^{25}\),
a quarter between \(2^{25}\) and \(2^{26}\), and so on. Promoting int64 with float32 to float32 can therefore lose integer precision.

> Simple examples

>> Views of a 3 by 4 matrix

Let \(x\) be the \(3\times4\) matrix with \(x_{ij}=4i+j\), stored row by row, so each value is also its storage position.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/tensor_basics_views.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

std::string shape_text(const nn::Shape& s) {
  std::string out = "(";
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) out += (i ? ", " : "") + std::to_string(s[i]);
  return out + ")";
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // The 3 x 4 matrix whose element (i, j) is 4i + j, stored row by row.
  const Tensor x = nn::reshape(nn::arange(0, 12), {3, 4});
  struct View { std::string expr; Tensor t; };
  const std::vector<View> views = {
      {"x", x},
      {"transpose(x)", nn::transpose(x)},
      {"slice_axis(x, 1, 0, 4, 2)", nn::slice_axis(x, 1, 0, 4, 2)},
      {"slice_axis(x, 0, 1, 3)", nn::slice_axis(x, 0, 1, 3)},
      {"broadcast_to(select(x, 0, 1), {3, 4})", nn::broadcast_to(nn::select(x, 0, 1), {3, 4})},
      {"sliding_window(flatten(x), 0, 4, 2)", nn::sliding_window(nn::flatten(x), 0, 4, 2)},
      {"reshape(transpose(x), {2, 2, 3})", nn::reshape(nn::transpose(x), {2, 2, 3})},
      {"reshape(transpose(x), {12})", nn::reshape(nn::transpose(x), {12})},
  };
  std::print("| expression | shape | strides | offset | contiguous | shares x's storage | storage bytes | values |\n");
  std::print("| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& v : views)
    std::print("| `{}` | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", v.expr, shape_text(v.t.shape()), shape_text(v.t.strides()), v.t.offset(),
               v.t.is_contiguous() ? "yes" : "no", nn::may_share_memory(v.t, x) ? "yes" : "no (copy)", nn::storage_bytes(v.t), v.t.values_str(12));

  // The original example's computations.
  const Tensor cube = nn::reshape(nn::arange(0, 4, 0.5, nn::DType::Float64), {2, 2, 2});
  const Tensor a = nn::tensor({{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}});
  std::print("\n| computation | result |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  std::print("| `cube.at({{1, 0, 1}})`, cube = arange(0, 4, 0.5) as [2, 2, 2] | {} |\n", cube.at({1, 0, 1}));
  std::print("| `a * 1.7` | {} |\n", (a * 1.7).values_str(8));
  std::print("| `cast(a * 1.7, Int32)` | {} |\n", nn::cast(a * 1.7, nn::DType::Int32).values_str(8));
  std::print("| `cast(-(a * 1.7), Int32)` | {} |\n", nn::cast(-(a * 1.7), nn::DType::Int32).values_str(8));
  std::print("| `a + tensor({{10, 20, 30}})`, [2, 3] + [3] | {} |\n", (a + nn::tensor({10, 20, 30})).values_str(8));
  std::print("| `tensor({{10, 20, 30}}) + tensor({{{{1}}, {{-1}}}})`, [3] + [2, 1] | {} |\n",
             (nn::tensor({10, 20, 30}) + nn::tensor({1.0, -1.0}, {2, 1})).values_str(8));
  std::print("| `norm(a)` | {:.6f} (√91 = {:.6f}) |\n", nn::norm(a).item(), std::sqrt(91.0));
  try { (void)(a + nn::tensor({1, 2})); } catch (const std::exception& e) { std::print("| `a + tensor({{1, 2}})`, [2, 3] + [2] | throws: {} |\n", e.what()); }
  std::print("| dtype of `int32 + float32`, `int64 + float32`, `bool + int64` | {}, {}, {} |\n",
             nn::dtype_name(nn::promote_types(nn::DType::Int32, nn::DType::Float32)), nn::dtype_name(nn::promote_types(nn::DType::Int64, nn::DType::Float32)),
             nn::dtype_name(nn::promote_types(nn::DType::Bool, nn::DType::Int64)));

  // Figure: each view of x drawn as its logical grid, each cell labelled with the storage position it reads.
  std::vector<dm::graphics::RPlot> panels;
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::string>> shown = {
      {0, "x"}, {1, "transpose(x)"}, {2, "every other column"}, {4, "row 1 broadcast to 3 rows"},
      {5, "windows of 4, step 2"}, {7, "transpose(x) flattened: a copy"}};
  for (const auto& [p, label] : shown) {
    const auto& v = views[p];
    Tensor t = v.t.dim() == 1 ? nn::reshape(v.t, {1, v.t.shape()[0]}) : v.t;
    const std::int64_t rows = t.shape()[0], cols = t.shape()[1];
    const bool copy = !nn::may_share_memory(v.t, x);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < rows; ++i)
      for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < cols; ++j) {
        const std::int64_t pos = t.offset() + i * t.strides()[0] + j * t.strides()[1];
        const double f = static_cast<double>(pos) / 11.0;
        const dm::graphics::RGB fill = copy ? dm::graphics::RGB{203, 213, 225}
                                            : dm::graphics::RGB{int(219 - 182 * f), int(234 - 135 * f), int(254 - 19 * f)};
        const double y = static_cast<double>(rows - 1 - i), xx = static_cast<double>(j);
        plot.polygon({xx + 0.04, xx + 0.96, xx + 0.96, xx + 0.04}, {y + 0.04, y + 0.04, y + 0.96, y + 0.96}, fill);
        plot.text(xx + 0.5, y + 0.36, std::to_string(pos), f > 0.55 && !copy ? dm::graphics::RGB{255, 255, 255} : dm::graphics::RGB{17, 24, 39}, cols > 6 ? 16 : 18);
      }
    plot.x_limits(0, std::max<std::int64_t>(cols, 4)).y_limits(0, std::max<std::int64_t>(rows, 3)).hide_axes();
    plot.title(std::format("{}, strides {}", label, shape_text(v.t.strides())));
    panels.push_back(plot);
  }
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(3, 2);
  for (auto& p : panels) fig.add(p);
  fig.size(1100, 900).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/tensor_basics_views.svg");
}
```
