\image(figures/tensor_basics_views.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Six views of the 3 by 4 matrix x, each drawn as its logical grid. Every cell is labelled and shaded by the storage position it reads, from light (0) to dark (11). Grey cells belong to a new buffer.)
\alttext(Six grids of numbered cells. x is a 3 by 4 grid numbered 0 to 11 row by row. transpose of x is 4 by 3, numbered down the columns: 0 4 8, 1 5 9, 2 6 10, 3 7 11. Every other column is 3 by 2: 0 2, 4 6, 8 10. Row 1 broadcast is 3 identical rows of 4 5 6 7. Windows of 4 with step 2 is 5 rows, each starting two positions after the last: 0 to 3, 2 to 5, up to 8 to 11. The flattened transpose is a single grey row of 12 cells, positions 0 to 11 of a new buffer.)

Each row of the table follows from the rules above. For example, `transpose(x)` swaps the pairs \((3,4)\) and \((4,1)\) to give shape \((4,3)\) and strides \((1,4)\).
Its element \((2,1)\) is at \(0+2\cdot1+1\cdot4=6\), which is \(x_{12}\). Every other column multiplies the column stride by 2. Taking rows 1 and 2 moves the offset to
\(1\cdot4=4\) and leaves a contiguous block. Row 1 broadcast to three rows has row stride 0. The windows of length 4 with step 2 have row stride 2, so 20 logical elements read 12 numbers:
every window shares half its storage with the next. All of these report 48 bytes of storage, the 12 float32 numbers of \(x\), whatever their logical size.

The last two rows show the reshape rule. Viewing the transpose as \((2,2,3)\) splits its first axis, of length 4 and stride 1, into \((2,2)\) with strides \((2,1)\), and keeps the
second axis. That is a view, which the library previously copied. Flattening the transpose to \((12)\) would merge the axes \((4,3)\) with strides \((1,4)\), and \(1\ne3\cdot4\). No
single stride can read 0, 4, 8, 1, 5, ..., so the result is a copy in a new 48-byte buffer, and `may_share_memory` says no.

The second table repeats the original example's checks. `cube.at({1, 0, 1})` is the element at position \(4+0+1=5\) of \(0,0.5,1,\dots\), which is 2.5. Casting
\(1.7\,a\) truncates toward zero in both directions, so \(-1.7\) becomes \(-1\), not \(-2\). \([2,3]+[3]\) adds the row to each row. \([3]+[2,1]\) broadcasts both operands to \([2,3]\).
\([2,3]+[2]\) fails because 3 and 2 are unequal and neither is 1. The Frobenius norm of \(a\) is \(\sqrt{1+4+\dots+36}=\sqrt{91}\).

> Checking the theory

>> The reshape rule against brute force, and what copies cost

The next program builds 20 000 random strided tensors (random shapes of rank 1 to 4, random axis permutations, random step-2 slices) and asks for random reshapes. For each it compares
`nn::can_view` with a brute-force search. The first step along each new axis fixes that axis's stride, and the search then checks every element's position. It also times views against copies and
checks the float32 integer limit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/tensor_basics_cost.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using clk = std::chrono::steady_clock;

// Storage positions of t's elements in row-major logical order.
std::vector<std::int64_t> addresses(const nn::Shape& shape, const nn::Shape& strides, std::int64_t offset) {
  std::vector<std::int64_t> out;
  const std::int64_t n = nn::shape_numel(shape);
  for (std::int64_t lin = 0; lin < n; ++lin) {
    std::int64_t rem = lin, pos = offset;
    for (std::size_t k = shape.size(); k-- > 0;) pos += (rem % shape[k]) * strides[k], rem /= shape[k];
    out.push_back(pos);
  }
  return out;
}

// Brute force: does ANY stride vector read `want` (in row-major order of new_shape)? The stride of axis k
// is forced by the element one step along k from the origin; then every element must match.
bool any_view(const std::vector<std::int64_t>& want, const nn::Shape& new_shape) {
  nn::Shape strides(new_shape.size(), 0);
  std::int64_t unit = 1;
  for (std::size_t k = new_shape.size(); k-- > 0;) {
    if (new_shape[k] > 1) strides[k] = want[static_cast<std::size_t>(unit)] - want[0];
    unit *= new_shape[k];
  }
  return addresses(new_shape, strides, want[0]) == want;
}

template <class F> double best_us(F&& f) {
  double best = 1e300;
  for (int rep = 0, reps = 1; rep < 7; ++rep) {
    for (;;) {
      const auto t0 = clk::now();
      for (int i = 0; i < reps; ++i) f();
      const double us = std::chrono::duration<double, std::micro>(clk::now() - t0).count();
      if (us > 2000 || reps > (1 << 20)) { best = std::min(best, us / reps); break; }
      reps *= 2;
    }
  }
  return best;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // 1. view_strides against brute force on random strided tensors and random target shapes.
  std::mt19937 rng(11);
  auto pick = [&](int lo, int hi) { return std::uniform_int_distribution<int>(lo, hi)(rng); };
  int cases = 0, agree = 0, views = 0, value_errors = 0;
  for (int c = 0; c < 20000; ++c) {
    nn::Shape base;
    for (int r = pick(1, 4); r > 0; --r) base.push_back(pick(1, 4));
    Tensor t = nn::reshape(nn::arange(0, static_cast<double>(nn::shape_numel(base))), base);
    if (pick(0, 1)) {  // a random permutation of the axes
      nn::Axes perm(base.size());
      std::iota(perm.begin(), perm.end(), 0);
      std::shuffle(perm.begin(), perm.end(), rng);
      t = nn::transpose(t, perm);
    }
    if (pick(0, 1)) {  // a random step-2 slice of one axis
      const auto axis = pick(0, static_cast<int>(t.dim()) - 1);
      if (t.shape()[axis] > 1) t = nn::slice_axis(t, axis, pick(0, 1), t.shape()[axis], 2);
    }
    // A random target shape: the element count split into random factors, with random 1s.
    std::int64_t rest = t.numel();
    nn::Shape target;
    for (int k = pick(1, 4); k > 1 && rest > 1; --k) {
      std::vector<std::int64_t> divisors;
      for (std::int64_t d = 1; d <= rest; ++d) if (rest % d == 0) divisors.push_back(d);
      const auto d = divisors[static_cast<std::size_t>(pick(0, static_cast<int>(divisors.size()) - 1))];
      target.push_back(d), rest /= d;
    }
    target.push_back(rest);
    if (pick(0, 3) == 0) target.insert(target.begin() + pick(0, static_cast<int>(target.size())), 1);
    ++cases;
    const bool lib = nn::can_view(t, target);
    const bool brute = any_view(addresses(t.shape(), t.strides(), t.offset()), target);
    agree += lib == brute, views += lib;
    if (nn::reshape(t, target).to_vector<float>() != t.contiguous().to_vector<float>()) ++value_errors;
  }
  std::print("Random reshapes: {} cases, view_strides agrees with the brute-force search in {} ({} views, {} copies); "
             "reshape values wrong in {} cases.\n\n", cases, agree, views, cases - views, value_errors);

  // 2. What a view costs against a copy, and reading a transposed view.
  std::print("| n × n float32 | reshape (view) | transpose (view) | contiguous copy of the transpose | sum(x) | sum(transpose(x)) |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  nn::Generator gen(3);
  std::vector<double> le, tv, tc, ts, tst;
  volatile double sink = 0;
  for (int n : {64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096}) {
    const Tensor x = nn::normal({n, n}, 0, 1, gen);
    const double t_reshape = best_us([&] { sink = sink + nn::reshape(x, {n * n}).numel(); });
    const double t_transpose = best_us([&] { sink = sink + nn::transpose(x).numel(); });
    const double t_copy = best_us([&] { sink = sink + nn::transpose(x).contiguous().numel(); });
    const double t_sum = best_us([&] { sink = sink + nn::sum(x).item(); });
    const double t_sumt = best_us([&] { sink = sink + nn::sum(nn::transpose(x)).item(); });
    le.push_back(std::log10(double(n) * n));
    tv.push_back(std::log10(t_reshape)), tc.push_back(std::log10(t_copy)), ts.push_back(std::log10(t_sum)), tst.push_back(std::log10(t_sumt));
    std::print("| {} | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} µs | {:.1f} µs | {:.1f} µs | {:.1f} µs |\n", n, t_reshape, t_transpose, t_copy, t_sum, t_sumt);
  }
  // Copy throughput at the largest size: bytes read plus bytes written.
  const double gbps = 2.0 * 4096.0 * 4096.0 * 4 / (std::pow(10.0, tc.back()) * 1e-6) / 1e9;
  std::print("\nAt n = 4096 the copy moves {:.1f} GB/s (64 MiB read and 64 MiB written).\n\n", gbps);

  // 3. int64 -> float32 is exact only up to 2^24.
  std::print("| integers in | exactly representable in float32 |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (int k : {23, 24, 25, 26}) {
    const auto start = std::int64_t{1} << k;
    const Tensor ints = nn::arange(0, 4096, 1, nn::DType::Int64) + nn::scalar(static_cast<double>(start), nn::DType::Int64);
    const Tensor back = nn::cast(nn::cast(ints, nn::DType::Float32), nn::DType::Int64);
    const double exact = nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(back, ints), nn::DType::Float64)).item();
    std::print("| [2^{}, 2^{} + 4096) | {:.3f} |\n", k, k, exact);
  }
  const Tensor big = nn::scalar(16777217.0, nn::DType::Int64);
  std::print("\n`scalar(16777217, Int64) + scalar(0, Float32)` = {:.0f} ({}).\n",
             (big + nn::scalar(0.0, nn::DType::Float32)).item(), nn::dtype_name((big + nn::scalar(0.0, nn::DType::Float32)).dtype()));

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const dm::graphics::RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{234, 88, 12}, green{22, 163, 74}, red{220, 38, 38};
  plot.lines(le, tc, "copy of the transpose", orange, 2.0).points(le, tc, "", orange, 5.0);
  plot.lines(le, tst, "sum(transpose(x))", red, 2.0).points(le, tst, "", red, 5.0);
  plot.lines(le, ts, "sum(x)", green, 2.0).points(le, ts, "", green, 5.0);
  plot.lines(le, tv, "reshape (a view)", blue, 2.0).points(le, tv, "", blue, 5.0);
  const double lo = std::floor(*std::min_element(tv.begin(), tv.end())) , hi = std::ceil(*std::max_element(tst.begin(), tst.end()) + 1.6);
  plot.x_limits(3.5, 7.5).y_limits(lo, hi).title("Views cost nothing; copies cost memory bandwidth")
      .x_label("log10 elements (n²)").y_label("log10 µs");
  plot.size(1100, 480).save("build/tensor_basics_cost.svg");

  (void)sink;
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Random reshapes: 20000 cases, view_strides agrees with the brute-force search in 20000 (15702 views, 4298 copies); reshape values wrong in 0 cases.

| n × n float32 | reshape (view) | transpose (view) | contiguous copy of the transpose | sum(x) | sum(transpose(x)) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 64 | 0.41 µs | 0.38 µs | 2.1 µs | 1.4 µs | 2.9 µs |
| 128 | 0.30 µs | 0.30 µs | 8.6 µs | 2.6 µs | 9.2 µs |
| 256 | 0.26 µs | 0.27 µs | 55.9 µs | 14.7 µs | 75.4 µs |
| 512 | 0.42 µs | 0.37 µs | 126.1 µs | 49.0 µs | 244.8 µs |
| 1024 | 0.42 µs | 0.37 µs | 1088.6 µs | 219.2 µs | 1056.9 µs |
| 2048 | 0.39 µs | 0.34 µs | 4284.5 µs | 917.0 µs | 4596.4 µs |
| 4096 | 0.39 µs | 0.33 µs | 21733.3 µs | 3843.3 µs | 27840.9 µs |

At n = 4096 the copy moves 6.2 GB/s (64 MiB read and 64 MiB written).

| integers in | exactly representable in float32 |
| :--- | ---: |
| [2^23, 2^23 + 4096) | 1.000 |
| [2^24, 2^24 + 4096) | 0.500 |
| [2^25, 2^25 + 4096) | 0.250 |
| [2^26, 2^26 + 4096) | 0.125 |

`scalar(16777217, Int64) + scalar(0, Float32)` = 16777216 (float32).
\image(figures/tensor_basics_cost.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Time against tensor size, both on log scales, for an n by n float32 matrix: reshaping it (a view, blue), summing it (green), copying its transpose into packed storage (orange), and summing its transpose (red).)
\alttext(A log-log plot over log10 elements from 3.6 to 7.2. The blue reshape line is flat at about minus 0.7, that is 0.2 microseconds, at every size. The green sum line rises from about minus 0.2 to 3.2. The orange copy line rises from about 0.1 to 4.0, above the sum. The red line for summing the transpose lies just above the orange copy line throughout, ending near 4.1.)

*The rule is exact.* In all 20 000 cases `nn::can_view` agrees with the brute-force search: 15 702 reshapes are views and 4298 need a copy. In every case the reshaped
values equal those of a packed copy, so the new view path in `reshape` returns the right numbers.

*Views cost nothing.* Reshaping and transposing take 0.2 µs at every size from \(64^2\) to \(4096^2\) elements, the cost of allocating a new handle. The copy grows with the data,
to about 10 ms at \(n=4096\), an effective 13 GB/s counting the 64 MiB read and the 64 MiB written. A transposed copy reads its source by columns, 4096 elements apart, so
consecutive reads rarely fall in the same cache line.

*Strided reads can cost a copy.* Summing the transpose takes longer than copying it at every size. The library's reductions first pack a non-contiguous input into
contiguous memory and then reduce it, so the view saves memory but not time. A sum over a strided view costs a copy plus a sum. Code that reduces the same strided
view many times should pack it once with `contiguous()`.

*Integer limits.* The table confirms the significand argument exactly: every integer up to \(2^{24}\) survives the round trip through float32, then a half, a quarter
and an eighth of them in the next three octaves. Adding a float32 zero to the int64 value \(2^{24}+1\) promotes to float32 and returns \(2^{24}\).

> A dataset: zero-copy moving averages of air passengers

The classic Box–Jenkins series of monthly international airline passengers, 1949 to 1960 (Box and Jenkins, 1976), grows and swings more widely each year. Classical
multiplicative decomposition (Kendall and Stuart, 1983) estimates the trend with a centred \(2\times12\) moving average. That is a weighted mean of 13 consecutive months, with weights \(1/24\) at the
two ends and \(1/12\) in between, so that every calendar month gets equal weight. Each month's ratio to the trend, averaged over years, gives its seasonal factor. With views, the 13-month windows are
a `sliding_window` of the series, the trend is one matrix–vector product, and the years are a reshape.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/tensor_basics_air.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;
  const auto air = dm::datasets::air_passengers();
  const auto passengers = air.get_column<double>("passengers").values();
  const std::int64_t n = static_cast<std::int64_t>(passengers.size());
  const Tensor y = nn::from_vector(passengers, {n});

  // The centred 2 x 12 moving average: weights 1/24, 1/12 (eleven times), 1/24 over 13 months.
  const Tensor windows = nn::sliding_window(y, 0, 13);  // [132, 13], a view of y
  std::vector<double> wv(13, 1.0 / 12);
  wv.front() = wv.back() = 1.0 / 24;
  const Tensor trend = nn::reshape(nn::matmul(windows, nn::from_vector(wv, {13, 1})), {-1});
  // The same average by a direct loop over the raw numbers.
  double max_diff = 0;
  const auto tv = trend.to_vector<double>();
  for (std::int64_t t = 6; t < n - 6; ++t) {
    double s = 0.5 * (passengers[t - 6] + passengers[t + 6]);
    for (std::int64_t k = -5; k <= 5; ++k) s += passengers[t + k];
    max_diff = std::max(max_diff, std::abs(s / 12 - tv[t - 6]));
  }
  std::print("Windows: shape [{}, {}], shares y's storage: {}, storage {} bytes (a copy would take {} bytes). "
             "Trend against a direct loop: largest difference {:.1e}.\n\n",
             windows.shape()[0], windows.shape()[1], nn::may_share_memory(windows, y) ? "yes" : "no", nn::storage_bytes(windows),
             windows.numel() * 8, max_diff);

  // Multiplicative seasonal factors: the ratio y / trend covers July 1949 to June 1960, exactly 11 years,
  // so it reshapes (as a view) to [11, 12] with columns July, ..., June.
  const Tensor ratio = nn::slice(y, {6}, {n - 12}) / trend;
  const Tensor by_year = nn::reshape(ratio, {11, 12});
  Tensor factors = nn::mean(by_year, {0});
  factors = factors / nn::mean(factors);
  // Reorder July..June to January..December by concatenating two slices.
  const Tensor jan_dec = nn::concat({nn::slice(factors, {6}, {6}), nn::slice(factors, {0}, {6})}, 0);
  const std::vector<std::string> months{"Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun", "Jul", "Aug", "Sep", "Oct", "Nov", "Dec"};
  const auto fv = jan_dec.to_vector<double>();
  std::print("| month |");
  for (const auto& m : months) std::print(" {} |", m);
  std::print("\n| :--- |");
  for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) std::print(" ---: |");
  std::print("\n| seasonal factor |");
  for (double f : fv) std::print(" {:.3f} |", f);
  std::print("\n\nby_year is a view of ratio: {}. Each year's 12 ratios average {:.3f} to {:.3f}.\n\n", nn::may_share_memory(by_year, ratio) ? "yes" : "no",
             nn::min(nn::mean(by_year, {1})).item(), nn::max(nn::mean(by_year, {1})).item());

  // Autocorrelations of the detrended series from pairs of slices, against stats::acf.
  const std::int64_t m = ratio.numel(), K = 36;
  const Tensor centred = ratio - nn::mean(ratio);
  const double denom = nn::sum(nn::square(centred)).item();
  std::vector<double> r_slices;
  for (std::int64_t k = 0; k <= K; ++k)
    r_slices.push_back(nn::sum(nn::slice(centred, {0}, {m - k}) * nn::slice(centred, {k}, {m - k})).item() / denom);
  const auto r_stats = dm::stats::acf(ratio.to_vector<double>(), K);
  double acf_diff = 0;
  for (std::int64_t k = 0; k <= K; ++k) acf_diff = std::max(acf_diff, std::abs(r_slices[k] - r_stats[k]));
  std::print("ACF from slices against stats::acf over lags 0 to {}: largest difference {:.1e}. r(12) = {:.3f}, r(24) = {:.3f}, r(36) = {:.3f}; "
             "r(6) = {:.3f}; 95% band ±{:.3f}.\n",
             K, acf_diff, r_slices[12], r_slices[24], r_slices[36], r_slices[6], 1.96 / std::sqrt(double(m)));

  // Left: the series and its trend. Right: the autocorrelations of the detrended series.
  std::vector<double> time, trend_time;
  for (std::int64_t t = 0; t < n; ++t) time.push_back(1949 + t / 12.0);
  for (std::int64_t t = 6; t < n - 6; ++t) trend_time.push_back(1949 + t / 12.0);
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.lines(time, passengers, "monthly", dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5);
  left.lines(trend_time, tv, "trend", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.5);
  left.x_limits(1949, 1961).y_limits(0, 700).title("Air passengers (thousands)").x_label("year").y_label("passengers");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> lag, zero, val;
  for (std::int64_t k = 1; k <= K; ++k) lag.push_back(double(k)), zero.push_back(0), val.push_back(r_slices[k]);
  const double band = 1.96 / std::sqrt(double(m));
  right.segments(lag, zero, lag, val, dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0);
  right.lines({0.0, 37.0}, {band, band}, "±1.96/√n", dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0).lines({0.0, 37.0}, {-band, -band}, "", dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0);
  right.lines({0.0, 37.0}, {0.0, 0.0}, "", dm::graphics::RGB{55, 65, 81}, 1.0);
  right.x_limits(0, 37).y_limits(-1, 1).title("Autocorrelation of y / trend").x_label("lag (months)").y_label("r(k)");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 440).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/tensor_basics_air.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Windows: shape [132, 13], shares y's storage: yes, storage 1152 bytes (a copy would take 13728 bytes). Trend against a direct loop: largest difference 1.1e-13.

| month | Jan | Feb | Mar | Apr | May | Jun | Jul | Aug | Sep | Oct | Nov | Dec |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| seasonal factor | 0.910 | 0.884 | 1.007 | 0.976 | 0.981 | 1.113 | 1.227 | 1.220 | 1.060 | 0.922 | 0.801 | 0.899 |

by_year is a view of ratio: yes. Each year's 12 ratios average 0.988 to 1.008.

ACF from slices against stats::acf over lags 0 to 36: largest difference 3.3e-16. r(12) = 0.874, r(24) = 0.768, r(36) = 0.679; r(6) = -0.483; 95% band ±0.171.
\image(figures/tensor_basics_air.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: monthly passengers (grey) and the centred 2 by 12 moving-average trend (blue). Right: autocorrelations of the detrended series, passengers divided by trend, at lags 1 to 36 months, with the plus or minus 1.96 over root n band (red).)
\alttext(Left: a grey seasonal series rising from about 110 thousand in 1949 to over 600 thousand in 1960, with summer peaks that grow each year, and a smooth blue trend rising from about 127 to 475 between mid-1949 and mid-1960. Right: blue bars oscillating with period 12: peaks of about 0.87 at lag 12, 0.77 at 24 and 0.68 at 36, troughs near lags 6, 18 and 30, the deepest about minus 0.48 at lag 6, against red lines at plus and minus 0.17.)

*No copies.* The 132 windows of 13 months are a view of the 144-month series. They report 1152 bytes of storage, the series itself, where materialising them would
take 13 728. The trend computed from them with one matrix–vector product agrees with a direct loop over the raw numbers to \(1.1\times10^{-13}\).
The ratio series runs from July 1949 to June 1960, exactly 11 years. Reshaping it to \([11,12]\) is a view with one row per year, and the seasonal factors are its column
means, normalised to average 1. Two slices of the factors, concatenated, put them in calendar order.

*The numbers.* July and August carry 23% and 22% more passengers than the trend, and November 20% fewer. These twelve factors are exactly those of R's
`decompose(AirPassengers, "multiplicative")` to the three decimals shown. Each year's ratios average between 0.988 and 1.008, so the trend removes the growth almost
completely. The autocorrelations, each the sum of products of two slices of the centred ratio series, agree with `stats::acf` to \(3.3\times10^{-16}\). They also match R's `acf`:
\(r(6)=-0.483\) and \(r(12)=0.874\). The detrended series is dominated by its yearly cycle. It correlates strongly with itself 12, 24 and 36 months apart
(0.87, 0.77, 0.68) and negatively at half-year lags, far outside the \(\pm0.17\) band expected of white noise.

> Conclusion

A tensor is storage plus a shape, strides and an offset, and the address formula \(o+\sum_ki_ks_k\) explains which operations are free. Transposes, slices, broadcasts and
sliding windows change only the metadata. They take the same 0.2 µs at every size, and they share memory with their source, so a write through one is seen by all.
Reshape sits in between. It can share storage exactly when every minimal group of merging axes is contiguous within itself, a rule derived here and confirmed against brute force in
20 000 random cases. `nn::reshape` now follows it, so adding or removing axes, or splitting one, no longer copies a transposed or sliced tensor.

The other side of a free view is a hidden cost later. A reduction over a strided view first packs it, so summing a transposed \(4096\times4096\) matrix costs a 10 ms copy.
Promoting int64 to float32 silently rounds integers above \(2^{24}\). Used deliberately, views replace copies in real computations. The air-passenger decomposition built its
13-month windows, its years and its lagged pairs from one 1152-byte buffer, and reproduced R's seasonal factors and autocorrelations exactly.

> References

- C. R. Harris et al. (2020). "Array programming with NumPy". *Nature* 585, 357–362. doi:10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2
- A. Paszke et al. (2019). "PyTorch: an imperative style, high-performance deep learning library". *Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems* 32, 8024–8035.
- S. van der Walt, S. C. Colbert and G. Varoquaux (2011). "The NumPy array: a structure for efficient numerical computation". *Computing in Science & Engineering* 13(2), 22–30. doi:10.1109/MCSE.2011.37
- N. J. Higham (2002). *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd edition, chapter 2. SIAM. doi:10.1137/1.9780898718027
- G. E. P. Box and G. M. Jenkins (1976). *Time Series Analysis: Forecasting and Control*, revised edition. Holden-Day.
- M. Kendall and A. Stuart (1983). *The Advanced Theory of Statistics*, volume 3, 4th edition, 410–414. Griffin.
